                    r3_invest_readout: `Committed up front:`,
                    r3_invest_unit: `k of €700k`,
                    r3_invest_hint: `Drawn from the €1.8M runway. What the right number is depends entirely on the model you chose.`,
                    r3_conversion_label: `What share of the ~600 clients does your plan need? (%)`,
                    r3_conversion_readout: `Conversion your revenue case assumes:`,
                    r3_conversion_pct: `%`,
                    r4_plant_label: `The plant costs €95,000 a month. What happens to it?`,
                    plant_reconfigure: `Reconfigure — keep only the capacity the new model actually uses`,
                    plant_keep: `Keep it running in full — it is paid for and it still earns`,
                    plant_leaseback: `Sale-and-leaseback — take €150k off the building now, rent back what you need`,
                    plant_closeall: `Close it — €180k to exit, and the fixed cost stops next month`,
                    r4_team_label: `Thirty-eight people were hired to run print jobs. What do you do with them?`,
                    team_retrain: `Retrain into recurring delivery — same people, new work, headcount roughly holds`,
                    team_hireproduct: `Hire product and engineering alongside them — buy the capability you lack`,
                    team_shrinkupskill: `Shrink and upskill — fewer people, more senior, higher value per head`,
                    team_asis: `Leave the team as it is until the new model tells you what work there will be`,
                    r4_designers_label: `Two senior designers have a written offer from a Paris agency.`,
                    designers_retain: `Match it — equity, a real role and a retention package (€120k from the runway)`,
                    designers_hope: `Say nothing and hope. Spend the €120k on the build instead.`,
                    designers_release: `Let them go and hire juniors once the new model is defined`,
                    r5_pitch_label: `Nine July. How do you put it to the board and the lead investor?`,
                    pitch_coherent: `As one model — show how the proposition, the revenue logic and the operation hold each other up`,
                    pitch_tradeoff: `As a trade-off — name exactly what you are giving up and why it is worth it`,
                    pitch_vision: `As a bet — lead with the size of the prize and ask them to fund the option`,
                    pitch_bolton: `As an addition — a new digital line beside a print business you are not proposing to touch`,
                    r5_risk_label: `Name the one risk you are asking them to accept with you.`,
                    risk_conversion: `Conversion — the plan needs a share of the 600 that we have not yet proved`,
                    risk_capacity: `Delivery — we have promised a service we are not yet staffed to deliver`,
                    risk_adoption: `Adoption — the product has to be good enough that they use it without us`,
                    risk_runway: `Cash — the plan has to work before the runway ends, with no second attempt`,
                    risk_none: `None material — the plan is robust to what we can foresee`,
                    r5_margin_label: `Operating margin you commit to at the end of year two (%)`,
                    r5_margin_readout: `Committed margin:`,
                    r5_margin_pct: `%`,
                    r5_margin_hint: `Different models earn different margins. Claiming one you cannot reach costs more than claiming a modest one.`,
                    r5_conviction_label: `How hard do you hold the line if the board pushes back?`,
                    r5_conviction_readout: `Conviction:`,
                    coach_default: `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_intro: `You have eight years of content for 600 SMEs, a plant that bills €95,000 a month whether you use it or not, and €1.8M of cash between here and the board meeting on 9 July. There is more than one company you can build out of that, and I will back any of them that holds together — a managed service, a platform, or a smaller house that charges more. What I will block is a digital promise bolted onto a print P&L, so pick one proposition and make every decision after it serve that one rather than an average of the three.`,
                    coach_r1_structural: `<strong>Then the new model has to repair all three at once.</strong> A commoditised proposition, an eroding margin and a fixed cost base do not get fixed one at a time, which is what makes this a redesign rather than a cost programme. Everything you choose from here is a claim about that cause.`,
                    coach_r1_margin: `<strong>Price and cost is the problem you can act on this quarter.</strong> It buys months, and months are worth having. It does not tell the board what this company sells in 2029, which is the question they put on the agenda.`,
                    coach_r1_demand: `<strong>Following the budget means competing where the budget already lives.</strong> The agencies that took that spend have been there ten years and are not slower than you. If that is the diagnosis, the plan has to say what you bring that they cannot.`,
                    coach_r1_mix: `<strong>Retreating to the work you still win is a real strategy with a known shape.</strong> It shrinks the company on a schedule you can forecast, and the board will want to see where the line stops. Name the floor, or they will assume there isn't one.`,
                    coach_r1_research_full: `<strong>€60k for a number you cannot use for another month.</strong> If the segmentation comes back below what your plan needs, you have bought the right to change your mind cheaply — that is what you are paying for, not the certainty.`,
                    coach_r1_research_none: `<strong>You kept the €60k and you will be quoting the conversion from memory.</strong> That is a defensible call while the client base is yours to read. It stops being defensible the moment you put a number in front of the board and cannot say where it came from.`,
                    coach_r2_broadplatform: `<strong>A platform that takes every order that comes is a job shop with a website.</strong> Self-serve only works once one group of people uses the same thing the same way every week; taking everything keeps the print cash arriving and delays the only part that compounds.`,
                    coach_r2_sharp: `<strong>Saying it publicly is the part that costs.</strong> You have just told the region which work you no longer want, and some of it will not come back if you change your mind. The board will want the replacement revenue on a date.`,
                    coach_r2_focus: `<strong>The old work pays for the build and competes with it for the same thirty-eight people.</strong> That is workable as long as you are explicit about whose weeks belong to which, and it stops being workable the first time a print deadline eats the new one.`,
                    coach_r2_band_full: `<strong>The segmentation is back, and it is about the proposition you just chose rather than the one you assumed.</strong> The share of the 600 genuinely reachable by this model is narrower than the client list — use the band you paid for when you set the number, instead of rounding it up to what the plan needs.`,
                    coach_r2_band_light: `<strong>Sixty interviews give you the direction, not the number.</strong> The sample is wide enough that your conversion assumption can sit inside it and still be wrong by half — treat it as a sanity check on the model you chose, not as evidence.`,
                    coach_r3_frontload: `<strong>You have booked recurring revenue as if it were invoiced on delivery.</strong> It is not: the cost of the build lands now and the money arrives a month at a time, and that gap has just taken another €300k out of the runway. Re-run the curve before the meeting.`,
                    coach_r3_tooslow: `<strong>You bill on delivery and then plan the cash as if it compounded.</strong> Work that is quoted and invoiced inside the period has no eighteen-month trough — modelling one makes your own plan look weaker than it is and tells the board you have not run the invoicing.`,
                    coach_r3_service: `<strong>The promise and the invoice now move in the same rhythm.</strong> What you have to survive is the gap between them: you staff the delivery months before the monthly billing covers it, and that gap is the whole cash question for a service.`,
                    coach_r3_platform: `<strong>A take-rate pays nothing until there is volume to take it from.</strong> The build finishes long before the volume arrives and the runway has to cover the months in between — that is the real number you are asking the board to fund.`,
                    coach_r3_craft: `<strong>Quoted, delivered, invoiced — your cash has no trough, it has a queue.</strong> That is a genuine advantage against a fourteen-month clock. What you have to defend is that the queue stays full at a price the commodity houses cannot match.`,
                    coach_r3_underinvest: `<strong>You have underfunded the one thing the whole plan rests on.</strong> What you keep in the runway you will spend in July explaining why the build is half-finished — and a half-finished build is the easiest thing in the world for a board to decline.`,
                    coach_r3_overinvest: `<strong>You have committed more than this model consumes.</strong> Capability the proposition never uses is capital you cannot put into the months between launch and the first payment, which is where this plan actually gets tested.`,
                    coach_r3_overconvert: `<strong>More of the 600 come across in your plan than this proposition can reach.</strong> Halve the number and see whether the revenue case still stands; if it does not, the assumption is doing the work the model should be doing.`,
                    coach_r4_retaincost: `<strong>€120k of runway to keep two people.</strong> Defensible — they are the only ones who know both the archive and the clients — but it is €120k that is no longer available to the build, and the board will net the two against each other.`,
                    coach_r4_hope: `<strong>You have decided to find out in September whether they were loyal.</strong> If they take the Paris offer, the capability leaves with them and the €120k you saved buys none of it back at short notice.`,
                    coach_r4_release: `<strong>The payroll gets lighter and the institutional memory walks out.</strong> Juniors can be hired at any time; eight years of knowing which client signs off on what cannot, and that was the part the new model was going to lean on.`,
                    coach_r4_twocostbases: `<strong>You are carrying the full €95k a month and hiring engineers on top of it.</strong> That is two cost bases inside one P&L, each waiting for the other to justify itself. The board will ask which one the plan is actually funding.`,
                    coach_r4_coherent_service: `<strong>The capacity you kept is the capacity a monthly service actually consumes, and the people running it already know the clients.</strong> The cost base now moves with the subscriptions instead of arriving ahead of them — which is the difference between a service and a print shop that invoices monthly.`,
                    coach_r4_coherent_platform: `<strong>The presses come off the balance sheet and product engineers go on it.</strong> That is the trade a platform requires, and it is irreversible in the time you have: there is no print revenue left to cushion a slow start, so the adoption curve has to be right first time.`,
                    coach_r4_coherent_craft: `<strong>Fewer, more senior people on presses you decided to keep is the only version of this that earns a premium.</strong> The €95k a month is a production cost now rather than a stranded one — for exactly as long as the order book justifies the machines.`,
                    coach_r5_coherent: `<strong>The parts hold each other up and you can demonstrate it rather than assert it.</strong> That is what gets a pivot funded off a declining P&L — the board is not buying the idea, it is buying the fit between the pieces.`,
                    coach_r5_overclaim: `<strong>You pitched one model and there are several in the room.</strong> They can see the seams from where they sit, and claiming a coherence you have not built costs more confidence than naming the gap would have.`,
                    coach_r5_tradeoff: `<strong>Naming what you are giving up runs the board's own argument before they can.</strong> It is the least comfortable way to present a plan and the hardest one to take apart.`,
                    coach_r5_vision_platform: `<strong>For a platform funded at this level, the size of the prize is the case.</strong> Nothing else about it pays back inside two years, so they are buying an option and you have priced it as one. Expect them to ask what that option costs to keep alive for a third year.`,
                    coach_r5_vision: `<strong>You asked them to fund an option on a plan that has to deliver inside fourteen months.</strong> The size of the prize does not answer the question they came with, which is when the cash arrives and what happens if it is late.`,
                    coach_r5_bolton: `<strong>A new line beside an untouched print business is the plan I told you I would block.</strong> Nothing in the operating logic changes, so nothing in the P&L changes either — you have added cost to the cost base that was already the problem.`,
                    coach_r5_risknone: `<strong>There is no version of this where nothing can go wrong.</strong> Saying otherwise tells the board either that you have not pressure-tested the plan, or that you have and would rather not say which part gave way.`,
                    coach_r5_riskright: `<strong>That is the one that can actually break this plan, and you named it before they did.</strong> Now they will ask for the version of the plan in which it happens — have that page ready.`,
                    coach_r5_riskwrong: `<strong>That is a real risk, but not the one this plan is most exposed to.</strong> A board notices the risk you left out far more reliably than it weighs the one you named.`,
                    coach_r5_overmargin: `<strong>You have committed to a margin this model does not produce.</strong> A number you miss in year two is remembered longer than a modest one you beat, and they will hold you to the slide.`,
                    coach_insolvent: `<strong>The cash ran out before the meeting.</strong> Whatever the model was worth is now academic — a plan that cannot be funded to its own break-even is not a plan, it is a proposal for somebody with more money.`,
                    sum_default: `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good: `Named the structural cause, not a symptom.`,
                    sum_r1_weak: `Diagnosed one symptom rather than the model.`,
                    sum_r2_service: `Committed to a service: you do the work, every month.`,
                    sum_r2_platform: `Committed to a platform: they use it without you.`,
                    sum_r2_craft: `Committed to craft: fewer jobs, higher price.`,
                    sum_r3_good: `Investment and conversion both sized to the model.`,
                    sum_r3_weak: `Investment or conversion out of step with the model.`,
                    sum_r4_service: `Operations judged against a promise that repeats monthly.`,
                    sum_r4_platform: `Operations judged against a product that runs without you.`,
                    sum_r4_craft: `Operations judged against short-run work at a premium.`,
                    sum_r5_service: `Asked the board to fund an annuity not yet built.`,
                    sum_r5_platform: `Asked the board to fund volume before liquidity.`,
                    sum_r5_craft: `Asked the board to fund a smaller, dearer company.`,
                    briefing_title_r1: `Step 1 — Diagnose before you redesign`,
                    briefing_text_r1: `Print revenue is down 12% a year, gross margin has gone from 34% to 22%, and the plant bills <strong>€95,000 a month</strong> whether or not anyone has decided what to do with it. Four readings of that are on the table and a competent board member would argue for any of them — the one you commit to is the one you will defend on 9 July. Then name the asset the reinvention is built on, and decide whether to buy the conversion research: it costs cash today for a number you cannot use until the monetization step, before you know which model you are choosing. <em>Deliberation is no longer free — the plant bills every step.</em>`,
                    briefing_title_r2: `Step 2 — Commit to one value proposition`,
                    briefing_text_r2: `Six propositions, built from the same assets, describing <strong>three different companies</strong>: one that runs the work for the client, one that sells them something they run themselves, and one that stays in print and charges more for less of it. Each can be made to work. None of them scores well on its own — from here on every decision is judged on whether it <em>fits the proposition you just committed to</em>, and the same choice is right under one and fatal under another. Then decide how much of today's business you keep serving while you build: the old work funds the build and competes with it for the same thirty-eight people.`,
                    briefing_title_r3: `Step 3 — Make the model pay`,
                    briefing_text_r3: `A revenue logic, a cash-flow shape, a sum of money and a number. The revenue logic has to match the promise and the cash shape has to match the revenue logic — <strong>revenue that recurs does not arrive the way an invoiced job does</strong>, and work delivered inside the period has no eighteen-month trough. Then commit part of the €1.8M up front against a €700k cap: too little and the build never reaches a standard anyone funds, too much and you have bought capability this model never uses. Last, the conversion bet — <em>what share of the ~600 the plan needs.</em> If you bought the segmentation you know roughly where the honest band sits; if you did not, a miss costs you considerably more.`,
                    briefing_title_r4: `Step 4 — Make the operation fit`,
                    briefing_text_r4: `The plant, the thirty-eight people, and two senior designers with a written offer from Paris. There is no universally right answer here: <strong>the same decision is sound under one proposition and fatal under another</strong> — presses a premium house needs are a stranded cost to a platform, and the product engineers a platform must hire are overhead a craft business cannot carry. The plant stops billing €95k a month only once you resolve it, and it has been billing since step one. Retaining the designers costs <strong>€120k of the same runway</strong> that funds the build; letting them go costs you the only people who know both the archive and the clients.`,
                    briefing_title_r5: `Step 5 — The board meeting`,
                    briefing_text_r5: `Nine July. Four ways to put it, and which one works depends on what you have actually built — <strong>claiming one coherent model when the parts contradict each other loses more confidence than it wins</strong>. Name the single risk you are asking them to carry with you: which one is honest is read off your own runway, capacity and conversion bet, not off a list. Commit to an operating margin your model can produce — <em>different models earn different margins</em> — and decide how hard you hold the line when they push back. Confidence below 70 and the board moves toward a sale.`,
                    round_name_r1: `Diagnose`,
                    round_name_r2: `Value Proposition`,
                    round_name_r3: `Monetization`,
                    round_name_r4: `Operational Coherence`,
                    round_name_r5: `The Board`,
                    clock_r1: `4 weeks to the board meeting · 9 July 2026`,
                    clock_r2: `3 weeks to the board meeting · 600 SME clients`,
                    clock_r3: `2 weeks to the board meeting · €1.8M runway`,
                    clock_r4: `1 week to the board meeting · €95k/month plant`,
                    clock_r5: `Board meeting today · 9 July 2026`,
                    subtitle: `A regional print house is dying — print revenue is falling 12% a year and the bank has just cut the overdraft. You are the founder, with €1.8M of runway, ~600 SME clients, eight years of their content and a plant that bills €95k a month whether you use it or not. Using Dauchy's 7-step discipline, commit to one value proposition and then make the revenue logic, the operation and the pitch serve that one — there is more than one company you could build here, and only the fit between the parts is scored.`
                },
